{
  "markdown_report": "SASRec with a small recent-context gate and multi-negative training is the strongest first experiment. This is a hypothesis; no benchmark improvement has been measured.\n\n**Insights from the starting point.** (1) SASRec already models ordered context, making a small extension feasible. Retaining its encoder limits implementation risk. (2) Electronics has 14.95% sampled wrong rankings and a −0.4812 tenth-percentile margin. This motivates stronger candidate discrimination, but does not establish HR@10 gains. (3) Users report both broad, unsupported recommendations and stale interests. Recent, repeated behavioral evidence could help both, although histories cannot establish ownership or compatibility.\n\n**Insights from related work.** (1) Dross Into Gold and gSASRec motivate testing richer negative sets before replacing the encoder. Their results do not establish an optimum here. (2) MISER and RecGRELA motivate separating recent and persistent preferences. A small gate offers a cheaper initial experiment than multiple interest encoders. (3) UFNRec cautions that difficult negatives can be unlabeled positives. Begin with uniform sampling and avoid interpreting uncertain recommendations as dislikes.\n\n**Framework and directions.**\n\n| Direction | Existing mechanism | Proposed test | Evidence gap |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Candidate discrimination | SASRec sampled loss; gSASRec | Uniform multi-negative softmax | Four-category HR@10 |\n| Current-interest evidence | MISER; RecGRELA | Gated recent residual | Immediate-choice acceptance |\n| Specific content evidence | Sceptre | Scratch-trained title residual | Compatibility remains unknown |\n\n**Candidate ideas.** Scores are originality / potential / user alignment / model alignment / implementation difficulty; difficulty increases with complexity. Each assessment gives a positive (+) and limitation (−).\n\n1. **Uniform multi-negative softmax:** 3/7/4/9/3. Originality: + grounded adaptation, − established; potential: + reusable, − benchmark-dependent; user: + finer ranking, − no explicit recency; model: + targets confusion, − false negatives; difficulty: + small loss change, − candidate masking.\n2. **Recent-evidence gate with multi-negative softmax:** **5/8/8/8/5**. Originality: + focused combination, − familiar components; potential: + adaptive context, − sparse-history uncertainty; user: + addresses stale and broad matches, − cannot verify practical fit; model: + trains competing candidates, − margins may not generalize; difficulty: + small module, − predictor parity required.\n3. **Scratch-trained title residual:** 4/6/7/6/6. Originality: + available-text adaptation, − established fusion; potential: + specific identity cues, − sparse vocabulary; user: + finer matches, − shared words can mislead; model: + additional discrimination, − shortcut risk; difficulty: + lightweight encoder, − tokenization artifacts.\n\n**Selected algorithm.** At 0% progress, choose idea 2. Fuse SASRec features with an attention-pooled recent prefix through a learned gate, then train against 64 uniform negatives. This explicitly targets stale/isolated-interest reliance and broad matches lacking recent support; multi-negative competition targets weak lower-tail separation. These are intended mechanisms, not verified outcomes.\n\nUse causal prefixes, exclude known training positives from negative pools, and preserve all category routing. Compare baseline, loss-only, gate-only, and combined variants within budget. Select exclusively by pinned macro validation HR@10. Keep diagnostic definitions explicit and regenerate the predictor with identical architecture.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Add a gated recent-context residual to each category's SASRec and train it with uniform multi-negative candidate softmax. At position t, attention-pool the last five valid prefix item embeddings, fuse that vector with the causal SASRec feature, and score candidates using the shared item embedding table. Keep separate category models and the existing routing.",
    "motivation": "The user diagnosis identifies stale or isolated interests and broadly related recommendations without specific current support. A learned recent-context branch directly introduces evidence relevant to both failures without claiming to observe ownership, compatibility, or intent. Candidate competition addresses the measured weak positive–negative separation, especially Electronics' negative tenth-percentile margin. Both effects require validation.",
    "implementation_notes": "Retain preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], all four datasets, trial-local artifacts, and the existing SASREC_EPOCHS environment behavior with default 200. Preserve the baseline encoder configuration initially. For each valid causal position, pool at most five preceding/current input tokens using a learned query; compute g = sigmoid(MLP([h, r, h*r, log1p(prefix_length)])), then z = LayerNorm(h + g*r). Initialize the gate bias to -2 to favor the starting encoder. Use z consistently in training, log2feats, and generated inference. Sample 64 unique uniform negatives per supervised position, excluding padding, the target, and that user's known training items; reduce the count if the eligible catalog is smaller. Do not consult held-out interactions for exclusions. Chunk supervised positions to bound memory; record sampling overhead. Use cross-entropy over the positive and sampled negatives, described as candidate softmax rather than an unbiased full-softmax estimator. Compare baseline, loss-only, gate-only, and combined variants sequentially on one GPU, stopping search before the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget is exhausted. Use only the existing pinned validation protocol for checkpoint and variant selection, reporting all four category scores plus their macro mean. Regenerate working/predict.py through the predictor writer with the same module, configuration, state, category routing, and deterministic evaluation behavior. Preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Probe view.model and fused log2feats/predict_score outputs; report measured margins with their sampling policy and definitions. Preserve existing diagnostic names where definitions remain unchanged. Categories, reviews, ratings, simulator traits, ownership, and compatibility must not be fabricated; unavailable metadata breakdowns should be explicitly unavailable. Near-0.5 mapped cosine scores do not justify an embedding-collapse penalty.",
    "pseudocode": "class RecentGatedSASRec(BaseSASRec):\n    def log2feats(self, sequence):\n        h = self.causal_encoder(sequence)\n        r = causal_recent_attention(self.item_emb(sequence), window=5)\n        length = causal_valid_prefix_lengths(sequence)\n        g = sigmoid(self.gate(concat(h, r, h * r, log1p(length))))\n        return self.fusion_norm(h + g * r)\n\nfor category in FOUR_CATEGORIES:\n    seed_training(42)\n    model = RecentGatedSASRec(baseline_config)\n    for epoch in range(int(os.getenv('SASREC_EPOCHS', '200'))):\n        for users, sequence, positive, _ in training_batches:\n            features = model.log2feats(sequence)\n            positions = valid_supervised_positions(positive)\n            for chunk in bounded_chunks(positions):\n                negatives = unique_uniform_training_only_negatives(\n                    users, chunk, count=64, exclude_padding=True,\n                    exclude_targets=True, exclude_known_training_items=True)\n                candidates = prepend_positive(positive[chunk], negatives)\n                logits = model.predict_score(features[chunk], candidates)\n                loss = cross_entropy(logits, target_column=0)\n                accumulate_correctly_weighted_gradients(loss, chunk)\n            optimizer.step()\n            optimizer.zero_grad()\n        checkpoint_using_existing_validation_protocol()\n\nselect_variant_by_pinned_four_category_macro_validation_hr10()\nwrite_predictor_with_identical_model_and_category_routing()\n# Diagnostic adapter evaluates fused features and returns measured metrics\n# together with definitions; its interpreter prompt includes both.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "Combines a small causal recent-evidence gate with richer candidate competition to target the supplied failure patterns.",
      "negative": "Recency fusion and multi-negative objectives are established ideas; novelty lies primarily in the constrained adaptation."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 8,
      "positive": "Provides an inexpensive starting point for testing when recent evidence should outweigh persistent preferences.",
      "negative": "Interaction recency alone cannot identify practical compatibility, satisfied needs, or immediate purchase intent."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "Retains SASRec and adds only a recent pooling module, gate, and candidate loss.",
      "negative": "Efficient history-aware sampling, causal masking, checkpoint compatibility, and predictor-writer parity require care."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
    {
      "title": "Self-Attentive Sequential Recommendation",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.09781",
      "contributions": [
        "Introduces the causal self-attention sequential recommendation architecture used as the starting point."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "The starting architecture does not explicitly distinguish satisfied needs, compatibility, or current intent."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Turning Dross Into Gold Loss",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.07602",
      "contributions": [
        "Supports investigating cross-entropy objectives and larger negative sets for SASRec."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish improvement on this pinned four-category benchmark or determine the proposed negative count."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "gSASRec",
      "link": "https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/939",
      "contributions": [
        "Uses additional negatives and generalized BCE to address sampling-induced overconfidence."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Its objective differs from the proposed candidate softmax and should be treated as a separate experimental alternative."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "RecGRELA",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.13315",
      "contributions": [
        "The supplied search summary describes local token mixing and gates balancing transient and stable preferences."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Motivates the recent-context gate without validating this particular SASRec adaptation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "MISER",
      "link": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0950705124012917",
      "contributions": [
        "The supplied search summary describes temporal analysis combined with multi-interest contrastive learning."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "A full multi-interest architecture adds complexity beyond the proposed initial experiment."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "UFNRec",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.04116",
      "contributions": [
        "Addresses suspected false negatives using teacher soft labels and consistency regularization."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not make unobserved items reliable dislikes or eliminate false-negative risk in uniform sampling."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Sceptre",
      "link": "https://cs.stanford.edu/people/jure/pubs/prodgraph-kdd15.pdf",
      "contributions": [
        "Provides precedent for using product text to study item relationships."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Its original inputs and relation supervision exceed the available task evidence; title overlap alone cannot establish compatibility."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
